body.proof_tex:
We prove a graph-combinatorial certificate and then an all-procedure Bayes lower bound.  All probabilities in the first part refer to the uniformly random simple labeled \(d\)-regular graph.

For a fixed graph define the endpoint-exposure sets of an assignment \(z\) by
\[
E_a(z)=\{i\in V:z|_{N_G[i]}=\boldsymbol a|_{N_G[i]}\},\qquad a\in\{0,1\}, \tag{9}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol a\) is the constant assignment with value \(a\).  If \(i\in E_1(z)\) and \(j\in E_0(z)\), then their closed neighborhoods are disjoint; otherwise a vertex in their intersection would be required to receive both treatment values.  Thus
\[
\operatorname{dist}_G(i,j)>2. \tag{10}
\]

We next establish the random-regular balanced-hole bound
\[
\Pr_{G_n}\!\left\{
\max_{z\in\{0,1\}^V}\min(|E_1(z)|,|E_0(z)|)
\le M\frac{n\log(d+2)}{(d+1)^2}
\right\}\longrightarrow1                                      \tag{11}
\]
for a universal constant \(M\).  Here is the required counting argument.  For fixed disjoint sets \(S,T\subseteq V\), \(|S|=|T|=t\), direct degree-preserving two-edge switchings give, whenever \(dt/n=o(1)\),
\[
\Pr\{\operatorname{dist}_{G_n}(S,T)>2\}
\le 2\exp\!\left(-c_0\frac{d^2t^2}{n}\right)                 \tag{12}
\]
for a universal \(c_0>0\).  For completeness, the switching count is as follows.  Expose the edges incident to \(S\), and write \(Q=N_{G_n}(S)\setminus S\).  If \(|Q|<dt/4\), choose a set \(Q_0\) of size at most \(dt/4\) containing \(Q\).  Replacing an edge constrained to \(S\cup Q_0\) and an oriented edge wholly outside \(S\cup Q_0\) by the crossed pair is a valid simple \(d\)-regular forward switching except for at most an \(O(dt/n+d/n)\) fraction of choices.  The reverse switching chooses its endpoint in \(S\cup Q_0\).  Comparing forward and reverse choices and summing over \(Q_0\) yields
\[
\Pr\{|Q|<dt/4\}
\le \sum_{q<dt/4}\binom nq
     \left(\frac{4(t+q)}n\right)^{dt/2}
\le \exp\{-c_1dt\log(n/(dt))\}.                              \tag{13}
\]
On \(|Q|=q\ge dt/4\), absence of a path of length at most two from \(S\) to \(T\) requires both \(T\cap(S\cup Q)=\varnothing\) and no edge between \(T\) and \(Q\).  A forward switching that crosses an oriented edge incident to \(T\) with an oriented edge incident to \(Q\) creates one \(T\)--\(Q\) edge.  After deleting choices that repeat a vertex or create an existing edge, at least \(c_2(dt)(dq)\) choices remain.  A graph with \(\ell\) such edges has at most \(2\ell nd\) reverse choices.  Therefore, for \(1\le\ell\le c_3dtq/n\), the level-count ratio satisfies
\[
\frac{|\mathscr G_\ell|}{|\mathscr G_{\ell-1}|}
\ge \frac{c_4dtq}{\ell n}.                                   \tag{14}
\]
If fewer than \(dq/2\) oriented edges at \(Q\) are available in this count, the same switching comparison applied to edges internal to \(S\cup Q\) bounds that exceptional level by \(\exp(-c_5dq)\).  Summing (14) over \(\ell\), using \(\sum_{\ell=0}^L x^\ell/\ell!\ge \exp(c_6x)\) for \(L\ge c_6x\), gives
\[
\Pr\{e(T,Q)=0\mid |Q|=q,\text{ the }S\text{-edge exposure}\}
\le 2\exp\!\left(-c_7\frac{dtq}{n}\right).                 \tag{15}
\]
All constants in (13)--(15) are uniform because \(dt/n=o(1)\) and \(d/n=o(1)\); the displayed exclusions account for loops and repeated edges, so these are switchings on simple regular graphs rather than an appeal to the unconditioned configuration model.  Since \(q\ge dt/4\), (15) has exponent at least \(c_7d^2t^2/(4n)\).  The exponent in (13) is larger because \(dt/n=o(1)\).  This proves (12).

Set
\[
t=\left\lceil M\frac{n\log(d+2)}{(d+1)^2}\right\rceil.       \tag{16}
\]
Assumptions \(\text{ass:degree-diverges}\) and \(\text{ass:subsqrt-degree}\) imply respectively
\[
\frac{dt}{n}=O\!\left(\frac{\log d}{d}\right)=o(1),
\qquad
t\longrightarrow\infty.                                     \tag{17}
\]
There are at most \(\binom nt^2\le(en/t)^{2t}\) ordered pairs \((S,T)\).  Equations (12), (16), and the union bound give
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Pr\{\exists S,T:|S|=|T|=t,\ \operatorname{dist}_{G_n}(S,T)>2\}
&\le 2\exp\!\left\{2t\log(en/t)-c_0d^2t^2/n\right\}\\
&\le 2\exp\!\left\{6t\log(d+2)-c_8Mt\log(d+2)\right\}=o(1) \tag{18}
\end{aligned}
\]
after choosing the universal \(M>7/c_8\).  If the maximum in (11) exceeded \(t\), (10) would supply such an \((S,T)\) after taking \(t\)-subsets.  Hence (18) proves (11).

Fix now a graph in the event (11), and retain \(t\) from (16).  We construct a prior supported entirely on \(\mathcal M_2(G)\).  Independently for \(a=0,1\), draw \(B_a\) uniformly from \(\{-1,1\}\).  Conditional on \(B_a\), draw independent \(\xi_{ia}\in\{-1,1\}\) with
\[
\Pr(\xi_{ia}=1\mid B_a)=\frac{1+\delta B_a}{2},
\qquad \delta^2=\frac1{4t}.                                  \tag{19}
\]
Define the schedule
\[
y_i(z)=
\begin{cases}
\xi_{i1},&z|_{N_G[i]}=\boldsymbol1|_{N_G[i]},\\
\xi_{i0},&z|_{N_G[i]}=\boldsymbol0|_{N_G[i]},\\
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}                                                    \tag{20}
\]
The cases in (20) are disjoint because \(N_G[i]\) is nonempty.  The value depends only on \(z|_{N_G[i]}\), so \(\text{ass:ani}\) holds.  Moreover \(y_{ia}^2=1\), whence
\[
n^{-1}\sum_i y_{ia}^2=1,
\]
for both arms.  Thus every realization of (20) belongs to \(\mathcal M_2(G)\), including its boundary.

Consider any outcome-independent assignment law and any possibly randomized estimator.  Conditional on its realized assignment \(z\), the data from (20) reveal exactly \((\xi_{i1}:i\in E_1(z))\), \((\xi_{i0}:i\in E_0(z))\), and constants equal to zero elsewhere.  The design and any estimator randomization are independent of the prior.  By (11), at least one arm, denoted \(a(z)\), has
\[
m=|E_{a(z)}(z)|\le t.                                         \tag{21}
\]

We bound the posterior uncertainty of that arm.  For \(m\) observations in (19), let \(P_+^{(m)}\) and \(P_-^{(m)}\) be their laws given \(B_a=1\) and \(B_a=-1\).  Their Hellinger affinity is computed exactly as
\[
\rho_m=\sum_x\sqrt{P_+^{(m)}(x)P_-^{(m)}(x)}
=(1-\delta^2)^{m/2}.                                          \tag{22}
\]
By Cauchy--Schwarz,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{TV}(P_+^{(m)},P_-^{(m)})
&=\frac12\sum_x|\sqrt{P_+^{(m)}(x)}-\sqrt{P_-^{(m)}(x)}|
                  (\sqrt{P_+^{(m)}(x)}+\sqrt{P_-^{(m)}(x)})\\
&\le\sqrt{1-\rho_m^2}
\le\sqrt{m\delta^2}\le\frac12,                              \tag{23}
\end{aligned}
\]
where the penultimate inequality uses \(1-(1-x)^m\le mx\), and the last uses (19) and (21).  If \(q\) is the posterior mean of \(B_a\), then under the equal mixture prior
\[
\mathbb E|q|=\operatorname{TV}(P_+^{(m)},P_-^{(m)}),
\quad |q|\le1,
\]
so (23) implies
\[
\mathbb E[\operatorname{Var}(B_a\mid\text{the }m\text{ observations})]
=1-\mathbb E[q^2]\ge1-\mathbb E|q|\ge\frac12.                \tag{24}
\]

Let \(L=n-m\) be the number of unobserved endpoints in this arm.  Conditional on \(B_a\), these endpoints are independent with variance \(1-\delta^2\) and mean \(\delta B_a\).  The conditional variance identity and (24) therefore give
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E\!\left[\operatorname{Var}\!\left(
 n^{-1}\sum_i\xi_{ia}\,\middle|\,z,\text{observed outcomes}
\right)\,\middle|\,z\right]
&=\frac1{n^2}\mathbb E\left[L(1-\delta^2)+L^2\delta^2
\operatorname{Var}(B_a\mid\text{observations})\right]\\
&\ge \frac{L^2\delta^2}{2n^2}.                               \tag{25}
\end{aligned}
\]
Because \(t/n=O(\log d/d^2)=o(1)\), eventually \(L\ge n/2\); hence (19) and (25) yield a lower bound \(1/(32t)\).

The two arm priors are independent and their likelihoods factor by arm.  Consequently the posterior variance of
\(\tau_G=n^{-1}\sum_i(\xi_{i1}-\xi_{i0})\) is the sum of the two arm posterior variances, and is at least the contribution (25) from arm \(a(z)\).  Conditional expectation minimizes squared error, so every estimator has Bayes risk at least \(1/(32t)\).  Since the prior is supported on \(\mathcal M_2(G)\), its Bayes risk is bounded above by the worst-case risk of the same design--estimator pair.  The prior did not depend on that pair, so taking the infimum over all outcome-independent designs and all measurable randomized estimators gives
\[
R_2(G)\ge\frac1{32t}
\ge c\frac{(d+1)^2}{n\log(d+2)}
=c r(n,d)                                                       \tag{26}
\]
for a universal \(c>0\).  Combining (11) and (26) proves the theorem with probability \(1-o(1)\), simultaneously for all procedures, including biased and randomized ones.
